The  utility  performs post‐installation checks and/or fixes on a
system’s configuration files.  It is especially useful after sys‐
tem upgrades, e.g. after updating from to The items to  check  or
fix are divided in two groups: enabled by default and disabled by
default.   The  latter are items that are dangerous for some rea‐
son, for example because they remove files which may be still  in
use.   If  no  are  provided, the default checks or fixes are ap‐
plied.  Those which are disabled by default must be provided  ex‐
plicitly.   Supported options: MACHINE_ARCH.  Defaults to machine
of the host operating system.  Destination  directory  to  check.
Defaults  to  MACHINE.  Defaults to machine of the host operating
system.  The location of the reference files, or the source files
used to create the reference files.  This may be specified in one
of three ways: The top level directory of the  source  tree.   By
default  this  is  A directory in which reference files have been
extracted from a binary distribution of The files that  are  dis‐
tributed  in the or set file must be present.  The files that are
distributed in the or set file are optional.  The location  of  a
set file (or or such as or from a binary distribution of Each set
file  is  a  compressed archive containing reference files, which
will be extracted to the directory.  Multiple options may be used
to specify multiple set files.  The set file must  be  specified.
The  set  file  is  optional.   Location of the X11 source files.
This must be a directory that contains a xsrc tree.  Display help
to stdout, and exit.  The argument may be one of:  Perform  post‐
installation  checks on items.  Similar to but also show the dif‐
ferences between the files.  Apply fixes that determines need  to
be  applied.   Not all items can be automatically fixed by and in
some cases an error will be reported, after which  manual  inter‐
vention  will  be  required.  Conflicts between existing files in
the target file system and new files from  the  distribution  are
resolved  by replacing the existing file with the new file; there
is no attempt to merge the files.  See for an alternative  update
method  that is able to merge files.  Display help to stdout, and
exit.  List available showing if they are enabled or disabled  by
default.   Display help to stdout, and exit.  The utility exits 0
on success, and >0 if an error occurs or  a  problem  was  found.
